psrdnoise
---------
app/src/main/res/raw/lib_psrdnoise2.glsl is vendored verbatim, with its licence
header intact, from:

  https://github.com/stegu/psrdnoise

  "Authors: Stefan Gustavson (stefan.gustavson@gmail.com) and Ian McEwan
   (ijm567@gmail.com). Version 2021-12-02, published under the MIT license.
   Copyright (c) 2021 Stefan Gustavson and Ian McEwan."

The full MIT text is in the file itself. It backs the curl-noise velocity field
(res/raw/curl_field_frag.glsl): psrdnoise returns an ANALYTIC gradient, which is
what makes that field exactly divergence-free rather than approximately so - the
field previously took central differences, and the error in a finite difference
IS the divergence that makes particles pile up. It also cut the field from
twelve noise evaluations per pixel to three, and its periodic mode is what a
seamless loop would be built on.

Scientific colour maps (cyclic)
-------------------------------
app/src/main/res/raw/cyclic_palettes.bin packs the five CYCLIC scientific
colour maps - bamO, brocO, corkO, romaO, vikO - at 256 entries each, RGB8,
3840 bytes for the set. Source distribution:

  https://github.com/callumrollo/cmcrameri  (cmcrameri/cmaps/*.txt)

whose LICENSE.txt states, in full attribution of the data as distinct from the
packaging:

  MIT License
  Colormaps in cmcrameri/cm/cmaps: Copyright (c) 2020 Fabio Crameri
  Python scripts and packaging: Copyright (c) 2020 Callum Rollo

Only the colour-map data is used; none of the Python is. The originals are
Fabio Crameri's Scientific colour maps (https://www.fabiocrameri.ch/colourmaps/),
whose citation is requested as a courtesy: Crameri, F. (2018), Scientific
colour maps, Zenodo, doi:10.5281/zenodo.1243862.

These are the CYCLIC members of the family specifically: their two ends join.
Measured on the packed atlas, the wrap gap is 0-2/255 against a largest
ordinary step of 3/255 - the seam is smaller than the distance between
neighbouring entries, which is what makes them safe for a circular quantity
where a hue ramp would band on lightness instead.
